## 5. Evaluation Metrics and Text Normalization

The benchmark must not confuse conventional character error rate with a custom Indic grapheme-level metric.

### 5.1 Unicode normalization

Before scoring:

- Normalize reference and prediction to Unicode NFC.
- Use the same normalization implementation and Unicode version for every candidate.
- Do not transliterate Marathi or Hindi.
- Do not convert Devanagari into Latin characters.
- Do not silently replace visually similar characters from different Unicode scripts.
- Preserve the original script and meaningful punctuation.

NFC normalization makes canonically equivalent Unicode sequences comparable. It does not correct spelling, alter words, or make incorrect OCR output correct.

### 5.2 Conventional Character Error Rate — CER

Conventional CER is calculated using Unicode code points:

`CER = (S + D + I) / N`

Where:

- `S` = substitutions.
- `D` = deletions.
- `I` = insertions.
- `N` = number of reference code points.

Use edit distance after NFC normalization.

For this benchmark:

- Include punctuation.
- Include whitespace.
- Report the exact scoring implementation.
- Do not label this metric as grapheme-cluster CER.

### 5.3 Grapheme-cluster CER

Indic scripts can contain multiple Unicode code points that visually form a single user-perceived character.

A separate grapheme-cluster metric will therefore be reported.

Procedure:

1. Normalize both texts to NFC.
2. Segment the text into Unicode extended grapheme clusters using Unicode Standard Annex #29.
3. Calculate Levenshtein edit distance over those clusters.
4. Divide by the number of reference grapheme clusters.

This is a custom grapheme-level evaluation metric and must be labelled **Grapheme CER**, not conventional code-point CER.

Official Unicode reference:
https://unicode.org/reports/tr29/

### 5.4 Word Error Rate — WER

`WER = (S + D + I) / N`

For WER:

- Normalize both texts to NFC.
- Convert consecutive whitespace sequences into a single separator.
- Split text into whitespace-delimited tokens.
- Preserve punctuation attached to tokens.
- Do not remove punctuation before scoring.
- Use the same tokenization procedure for every model.

This makes the scoring reproducible and prevents different providers from receiving different scoring treatment.

### 5.5 Punctuation evaluation

Because punctuation must not be automatically corrected, punctuation errors must be visible in the report.

Report:

- Missing punctuation.
- Extra punctuation.
- Incorrect punctuation substitutions.
- Punctuation attached to the wrong token, where identifiable.

Punctuation remains part of the main text scoring. A separate punctuation error breakdown is an additional diagnostic.

### 5.6 `[illegible]` scoring policy

- If the ground truth marks a span as `[illegible]`, do not treat the unknown original word as known text.
- Exclude that span from readable-text CER/WER scoring through a documented reference mask.
- Report the number and proportion of masked reference spans separately.
- If a model marks a readable ground-truth word as `[illegible]`, count that as a recognition failure.
- If a model invents a word where the reference is unreadable, record it as an unsupported guess.

The masking process must be documented and applied identically to all candidates.

### 5.7 Additional evaluation measures

| Measure | Purpose |
|---|---|
| CER | Character-level recognition errors |
| Grapheme CER | Indic grapheme-level recognition errors |
| WER | Word-level recognition errors |
| Language fidelity | Detects unintended language changes |
| Hallucination count | Counts words not supported by the source |
| `[illegible]` behavior | Checks whether uncertainty is handled honestly |
| Processing time | Measures time per page |
| Cost per page | Measures provider and infrastructure cost |
| Formatting preservation | Checks paragraphs, headings, bullets, and tables |

