## 9. Provisional System Architecture

**Architecture status: PROVISIONAL — NOT APPROVED.**

No provider, backend framework, database, cloud-storage service, or hosting platform is locked in this phase.

The architecture must be evaluated after the OCR benchmark and technical feasibility tests.

### 9.1 Earlier backend terminology clarification

The earlier phrase **"Node.js Express + TSX"** was ambiguous.

It may have been intended to mean:

- Node.js as the runtime.
- Express as the backend HTTP framework.
- TypeScript as the programming language.
- `.tsx` files only where JSX syntax is required, typically for React components.

TSX is not itself a backend framework.

This interpretation is not an approved technology decision. The final backend stack must be selected after comparing implementation complexity, deployment needs, maintainability, security, and operating cost.

### 9.2 Architecture areas to evaluate

| Area | Evaluation requirement |
|---|---|
| Frontend | Simple, responsive blue-and-white interface |
| Authentication | Secure individual user accounts |
| Backend | Evaluate suitable server-side framework |
| OCR processing | Provider-neutral integration approach |
| Background processing | Suitable for longer document jobs |
| Database | Secure storage of user and project metadata |
| File storage | Private original uploads and generated documents |
| Document editor | Editable transcription and chapters |
| PDF generation | Verified Devanagari shaping and searchable text |
| DOCX generation | Verified Word and LibreOffice compatibility |
| Security | Per-user access isolation |
| Backups | Database and file recovery strategy |
| Monitoring | Processing failures, latency, and cost visibility |

### 9.3 Privacy and data isolation

The following are mandatory product requirements:

- A user can access only their own projects and notes.
- A user cannot access another user's uploaded documents or generated output.
- No shared workspaces.
- No institutional access to an individual's private notes.
- No unnecessary administrator access to note contents.
- Storage must be private by default.
- File access must be authorized server-side.
- Temporary processing files must follow a defined deletion policy.
- Account and project deletion must be considered in the data lifecycle.
- Backups must be protected and have a documented retention policy.

These requirements must be reflected in the architecture and security tests.

